<h2>Proximity drives this trade, the same as it does for onion and lentils</h2>
<p>Bangladesh takes roughly USD 125 million of Indian dried red chilli a year, and the underlying logic is the same structural advantage that makes India the default supplier for so much of Bangladesh's staple and spice basket: short transit keeps freight cost low relative to landed price. Where China's chilli demand is driven by industrial food-processing scale and the US market by a mix of ethnic and mainstream retail, Bangladesh's chilli trade looks much more like ordinary neighbouring-country culinary supply, at real staple-basket volume.</p>

<h2>Sea to Chattogram or overland — a real routing decision</h2>
<p>As with other Indian exports into Bangladesh, chilli can move by container to Chattogram (2-5 days from Indian east-coast ports) or overland through land ports such as Petrapole-Benapole. The right choice depends more on your sourcing region and order volume than on any chilli-specific factor — tell us where you're sourcing from and how much you need, and we'll recommend the route rather than defaulting to sea freight out of habit.</p>

<h2>SAFTA is worth checking, not assuming</h2>
<p>Qualifying shipments can access preferential SAFTA tariff rates with a valid certificate of origin, which is genuinely worth confirming rather than leaving on the table — but eligibility and the applicable rate are determined on the Bangladesh customs side, so this is a question for your buyer's customs broker, with us supplying the origin documentation that supports the claim.</p>

<h2>Plan from LC-operative date, not order date</h2>
<p>Bangladesh trade runs overwhelmingly on letters of credit, shaped by Bangladesh Bank regulation on how and when they're opened. The most common scheduling mistake we see is buyers treating the shipment timeline as starting from order confirmation rather than from when the LC actually becomes operative — build that gap into your planning explicitly.</p>

